```java
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8);

// Three independent fetches we want to combine
Future<User> userF = pool.submit(() -> userService.fetch(id));
Future<List<Order>> ordersF = pool.submit(() -> orderService.fetch(id));
Future<Prefs> prefsF = pool.submit(() -> prefsService.fetch(id));

// To combine them, we MUST block
User user = userF.get();       // blocks
List<Order> orders = ordersF.get(); // blocks
Prefs prefs = prefsF.get();    // blocks

UserDashboard result = build(user, orders, prefs);
// The caller thread sat idle during three network calls.
```

The more complex the flow (branching, exception recovery, timeouts), the more nested try/catches, `ExecutionException` unwrapping, and blocking `get()`s you accumulate. Future doesn't scale to real async code.

```java
Executor pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8);

CompletableFuture<User> userF =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> userService.fetch(id), pool);
CompletableFuture<List<Order>> ordersF =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> orderService.fetch(id), pool);
CompletableFuture<Prefs> prefsF =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> prefsService.fetch(id), pool);

CompletableFuture<UserDashboard> dashboardF =
    userF.thenCombine(ordersF, (u, o) -> new Object[]{u, o})
         .thenCombine(prefsF, (uo, p) -> build(
             (User) ((Object[]) uo)[0],
             (List<Order>) ((Object[]) uo)[1], p))
         .orTimeout(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
         .exceptionally(ex -> UserDashboard.empty());

// Caller can return dashboardF immediately, never blocks
dashboardF.thenAccept(this::renderResponse);
```

Everything runs in parallel, combines when all complete, times out at 2 s, recovers from errors — all declaratively. The caller never blocks.

**1. Simple boolean flag**:
```java
private volatile boolean running = true;

public void stop() { running = false; }

private void workerLoop() {
    while (running) {  // sees updated value without needing lock
        processNext();
    }
}
```
Only one field, single-writer (stop) and multi-reader (workers) — visibility is all you need.

**2. Double-checked locking**:
```java
private static volatile Singleton instance;
// volatile prevents reorder of constructor and reference assignment
```

**3. Publishing an immutable reference**:
```java
private volatile Config config; // replaced occasionally, otherwise read-only

public void reloadConfig(Config c) { config = c; }
public Config getConfig() { return config; }
```
Immutability of `Config` is key — readers see a fully-constructed object; the reference swap is atomic.

**4. Last-write-wins single-value state**:
```java
private volatile long lastHeartbeat;

public void heartbeat() { lastHeartbeat = System.currentTimeMillis(); }
```
No multiple-field invariants, just a scalar.

**1. Increment counter — the canonical bug**:
```java
private volatile int counter = 0;
public void increment() { counter++; }
// BROKEN: counter++ = read counter, add 1, write counter
// Two threads can both read 5, both write 6; you lost an increment.
```
**Fix**: `AtomicInteger` with `.incrementAndGet()`, or `synchronized`, or `LongAdder` for high contention.

**2. Check-then-act**:
```java
private volatile Map<String, String> cache = new HashMap<>();
public String getOrLoad(String key) {
    if (!cache.containsKey(key)) {    // thread A sees missing
        String value = load(key);
        cache.put(key, value);         // thread B just put a different value
    }
    return cache.get(key);
}
// volatile on the reference doesn't help — the map itself isn't thread-safe
// and the check-then-act isn't atomic
```
**Fix**: `ConcurrentHashMap` + `computeIfAbsent`.

**3. Multi-field consistency**:
```java
private volatile int x = 0;
private volatile int y = 0;
public void setBoth(int newX, int newY) {
    x = newX;  // Another thread can observe x updated, y not yet
    y = newY;
}
```
**Fix**: synchronized block, or encapsulate (x, y) in an immutable pair and volatile-publish the whole thing.

**Platform thread — the classic way**:
```java
// Unbounded — avoid in practice
Thread t = new Thread(() -> handleRequest());
t.start();

// Bounded via ExecutorService — production pattern
ExecutorService pool = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
    50, 50, 0, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS,
    new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(500),
    new ThreadFactoryBuilder().setNameFormat("worker-%d").build());
pool.submit(() -> handleRequest());
```

**Virtual thread — three idiomatic forms**:
```java
// 1. Builder
Thread vt = Thread.ofVirtual().name("vt-", 0).start(() -> handleRequest());

// 2. Executor (recommended for tasks)
try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
        executor.submit(() -> fetch(i));
    }
} // close() waits for all tasks

// 3. One-liner
Thread.startVirtualThread(() -> handleRequest());
```

**Spring Boot 3.2+**:
```
spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true
```
Makes the Tomcat/Jetty request executor a virtual-thread-per-task executor. Existing `@RestController` code is unchanged.

**Detecting at runtime**:
```java
Thread.currentThread().isVirtual() // true for virtual, false for platform
```
